      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-99185",
      ":question/problem": "In a particular region, the average number of storms in September over the past decade was $5$. A meteorologist wants to test if the number of storms has changed. Let $X$ represent the number of storms in September this year. Under the null hypothesis that the average number of storms hasn't changed, what is the critical region of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $2.5%$?\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the cumulative distribution function of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\u22ef$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $\u22ef$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0067$ | $0.0404$ | $0.1247$ | $\u22ef$ | $0.9682$ | $0.9863$ | $0.9945$ | $\u22ef$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."
